/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Technology: behind every tap and click */
WB.L("touchscreens", "technology", "beginner", "How touchscreens feel your finger",
  "Your phone screen has no buttons, yet it knows exactly where you tapped, swiped or pinched. The secret is that your finger is part of an electrical circuit.",
  [
    ["Press to connect", "A touchscreen is a transparent sensor over a display, and older resistive screens work when pressure pushes two conductive layers together.",
      "Pick up your phone and tap an app. The glass does not move or click, yet the phone responds instantly. A touchscreen is really two things stacked together: a display that shows pictures, and a transparent sensor on top that works out where your fingers are, measuring tiny electrical changes many times a second.\n\nThe first touchscreens most people met were resistive, on early handheld organisers, GPS units, some ATMs and signature pads. Two thin, flexible conductive layers sit separated by a tiny gap. When you press, the top layer touches the bottom one, completing a circuit at that spot. Resistive screens work with a fingernail, a gloved hand or a stylus. But they need a firm press, feel spongy and traditionally handled one touch at a time."],
    ["Your finger as a conductor", "Modern capacitive screens detect how your conductive fingertip disturbs a small electric field in a grid of transparent electrodes.",
      "Almost every modern smartphone uses a capacitive screen. Your body conducts electricity, mainly because it is full of salty water. Beneath the glass sits a fine grid of transparent electrodes, usually made from indium tin oxide, which conducts electricity while letting light through. The grid holds a small electric field.\n\nWhen your fingertip comes close, it disturbs that field and changes the capacitance, the ability of nearby conductors to store electric charge, at the points nearest your finger. You do not need to press at all; a light brush is enough. That is why capacitive screens feel so responsive, and why they are covered by tough, smooth glass rather than soft plastic that has to bend."],
    ["Scanning the grid", "A controller chip scans a grid of electrodes many times a second to pinpoint every finger, and software works out what you meant.",
      "The electrodes are laid out in rows and columns, like lines on graph paper. A controller chip rapidly tests each crossing, measuring how much its capacitance has shifted. Where a finger rests, a cluster of crossings changes, and the chip calculates the cluster's centre. Because each crossing is measured separately, the screen tracks several fingers at once. This projected capacitance is what makes multi-touch gestures such as pinch to zoom possible.\n\nThe sensor only reports where fingers are; software decides what you meant. It tells a tap from a long press by timing, a swipe from a scroll by speed, and a pinch by tracking two points. Tablets use palm rejection to ignore your hand, and some phones add haptic feedback, a tiny vibration that fools your fingertip into feeling a click."],
    ["Gloves and raindrops", "Gloves insulate your fingertip so the screen cannot sense it, while water droplets can mimic a touch.",
      "Capacitive screens explain some everyday frustrations. Ordinary woollen or leather gloves insulate your fingertip, so the screen cannot sense it. That is why touchscreen gloves have conductive thread woven into the fingertips. A standard plastic stylus does nothing for the same reason, so styluses for phones have conductive tips.\n\nWater is the opposite problem. Droplets on the glass conduct enough to disturb the field, so a wet screen may register phantom taps or ignore real ones. Manufacturers use software to tell the small, steady signal of a raindrop from the moving signal of a finger, and some phones have a wet touch mode. Even so, using your phone in heavy rain or underwater remains unreliable for most people."],
    ["From radar rooms to pockets", "Capacitive touchscreens date back to the 1960s, but the 2007 iPhone made them mainstream.",
      "Touchscreens are older than many people assume. In the mid 1960s British engineer E. A. Johnson, at the Royal Radar Establishment in Malvern, described a capacitive touchscreen intended for air traffic control displays. In the early 1970s engineers at CERN, the European physics laboratory near Geneva, built capacitive touchscreens to control a particle accelerator. Resistive screens developed around the same era and spread through industry, kiosks and handheld gadgets.\n\nThe big leap into everyday life came in 2007, when Apple launched the first iPhone with a multi-touch capacitive screen designed for fingers rather than a stylus. Within a few years, physical keyboards on phones had largely disappeared, and swiping had become something even toddlers attempt on picture books."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes a resistive touchscreen register a touch?", ["Heat from your fingertip warming the glass","Pressing two conductive layers together","Light being blocked by your finger","Sound waves crossing the glass"], 1, "Pressing completes a circuit between two layers, which is why a stylus or gloved hand works."],
    [1, "Why can your finger operate a capacitive screen?", ["Your skin is slightly magnetic","Your fingertip reflects infrared light","Your fingerprint ridges grip the glass","Your body conducts a little electricity"], 3, "A conductive fingertip changes the capacitance at the points nearest it."],
    [2, "What does projected capacitance make possible?", ["Tracking several fingers at once","Working through thick gloves","Charging the phone via the screen","Brighter, sharper display colours"], 0, "Each electrode crossing is measured separately, so multiple touches can be told apart."],
    [3, "Why do touchscreen gloves work when ordinary gloves do not?", ["They are thinner than ordinary gloves","They warm the screen as you touch","They have conductive thread in the fingertips","They contain tiny magnets"], 2, "Conductive fingertips let the electrical effect of your finger reach the screen."],
    [4, "What was different about the 2007 iPhone's screen compared with many earlier ones?", ["It relied on a stylus for input","It was a multi-touch capacitive screen","It only responded to a gloved hand","It was the first touchscreen ever made"], 1, "Multi-touch capacitive screens designed for fingers made touch interfaces mainstream."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your phone feels your finger without a single button. It is borrowing from you."});

WB.L("how-wifi-works", "technology", "beginner", "How Wi-Fi works (and an Aussie connection)",
  "Every time you stream a show on the couch, your devices are holding a fast, invisible radio conversation. Part of the technology that makes it reliable came from Australian radio astronomers.",
  [
    ["Radio on two lanes", "Wi-Fi is a set of shared rules for sending data by radio, mainly on the 2.4 and 5 gigahertz bands.",
      "Sit in a café and you are surrounded by conversations you cannot hear. Phones, laptops and smart speakers all talk to wireless routers by radio. Wi-Fi is simply a set of agreed standards that let devices from different companies exchange data through the air. The name is a brand, coined in 1999, and it does not officially stand for anything; \"wireless fidelity\" is a popular myth.\n\nIt is the same basic physics as FM radio, just at higher frequencies. Most Wi-Fi uses around 2.4 gigahertz and 5 gigahertz, and newer gear can use 6 gigahertz where allowed. Each band is divided into channels, like motorway lanes. The 2.4 gigahertz band travels further and passes through walls better, while higher bands carry more data but fade faster, so modern routers usually offer both."],
    ["Router and shared channel", "Your router links your devices to the internet, and devices sharing a channel take turns, which is why crowded networks feel slow.",
      "The box in your hallway is several devices in one. A modem part connects to your internet service, such as the NBN. A router part gives each device a local address and directs traffic. An access point part converts data to Wi-Fi signals and back.\n\nA channel is shared space, like a room where only one person can speak clearly at a time. Devices listen before they transmit, and if the channel is busy they wait a short, random time. Data travels in packets, the receiver confirms each one, and lost ones are resent. When many devices and neighbouring networks crowd the same channel, everyone waits longer. That is why an apartment block in the evening can feel sluggish even on a fast plan."],
    ["Why the signal drops", "Distance, brick, concrete, metal, water and interference all weaken Wi-Fi signals, and echoes cause trouble too.",
      "Radio waves spread out and weaken with distance, and lose strength passing through obstacles. Brick, concrete and metal block Wi-Fi especially well, which is why a router in one corner of a double brick house may struggle to reach the far bedroom. Water absorbs these frequencies too, so fish tanks and even crowds of people can dampen a signal. Mirrors and metal appliances reflect it.\n\nThere is also interference. Microwave ovens work at roughly the same frequency as 2.4 gigahertz Wi-Fi, so heating leftovers can briefly disrupt a nearby connection. And signals bounce off walls and furniture, arriving several times with slight delays, a problem that turned out to be crucial in Wi-Fi's history."],
    ["The CSIRO breakthrough", "CSIRO scientists helped solve the indoor echo problem, and their patent earned Australia hundreds of millions of dollars.",
      "In the early 1990s a CSIRO team led by radio astronomer John O'Sullivan set out to make fast wireless networking work indoors. The obstacle was echoes: signals bouncing around a room smeared into each other and scrambled the data.\n\nDrawing partly on techniques for sharpening faint signals in radio astronomy, the team developed a way of spreading data across many frequencies and correcting for the echoes. CSIRO patented the approach, and it became important to fast Wi-Fi standards. CSIRO later took legal action against major technology companies over the patent, and the settlements brought in hundreds of millions of dollars. Many Australians proudly call Wi-Fi an Aussie invention, though in truth it combined work from many people and countries."],
    ["Better Wi-Fi at home", "Place the router centrally and in the open, update it, and secure it with a strong password.",
      "A few habits improve most home networks. Put the router somewhere central and elevated, out in the open rather than in a cupboard, behind the television or on the floor. Keep it away from microwaves and large metal objects.\n\nIn bigger or multi-storey homes, a mesh system uses several units that talk to each other to blanket the house, which usually works better than a single powerful router. Restart and update your router occasionally, since manufacturers fix bugs and security holes. Protect your network with a strong password and the newest security option your devices support, typically WPA3 or WPA2.\n\nFinally, Wi-Fi only covers the last few metres. If your internet plan itself is slow, no router can fix that."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is true about the 2.4 gigahertz Wi-Fi band compared with higher bands?", ["It always carries much more data","It cannot pass through any walls","It reaches further through walls","It is reserved for microwave ovens"], 2, "Lower frequencies reach further, while higher bands carry more data but fade faster."],
    [1, "What do Wi-Fi devices do before transmitting on a shared channel?", ["Raise their power to drown out others","Listen to check whether the channel is busy","Switch to a cable connection","Ask the internet provider for permission"], 1, "Listening and waiting a random time avoids collisions, but crowded channels still slow everyone down."],
    [2, "Why can a microwave oven interfere with Wi-Fi?", ["It shares the router's power circuit","It permanently jams all radio waves","It automatically resets nearby routers","It operates near the same frequency"], 3, "Overlapping frequencies mean a running microwave can briefly disrupt nearby connections."],
    [3, "What problem did the CSIRO team help solve?", ["Echoes scrambling indoor signals","Batteries draining too quickly","Viruses spreading over networks","Routers overheating in summer"], 0, "They found a way to spread data across many frequencies and correct for echoes."],
    [4, "Where is the best place for a home router?", ["Inside a cupboard, out of sight","Somewhere central, raised and open","Behind the television on the floor","Next to the microwave oven"], 1, "Open, central, raised placement avoids obstacles and sources of interference."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Heating leftovers can briefly disrupt your Wi-Fi. Here is the physics."});

WB.L("the-cloud", "technology", "beginner", "Where the cloud really lives",
  "Your photos, emails and streamed shows are not floating in the sky. They live in giant, humming buildings full of computers, protected by fences, guards and biometric checks.",
  [
    ["Someone else's computer", "The cloud means using computers owned by someone else, kept in warehouse-sized data centres full of servers.",
      "Take a photo and a copy may be backed up to the cloud within moments. The word suggests something weightless, but the reality is metal, cables and electricity. The cloud simply means using computers that belong to another company, reached over the internet. When you check email in a browser, stream a show or save to an online drive, the work and storage happen in a data centre, possibly thousands of kilometres away.\n\nA large data centre looks like a library where the shelves are computers. Tall racks hold servers, computers without screens or keyboards, stacked like pizza boxes. Fibre optic lines, often from several providers, link it to the internet. Hyperscale sites can hold tens of thousands of servers, protected by fences, guards, mantraps and biometric checks."],
    ["Keeping machines cool", "Servers turn electricity into heat, so cooling is one of a data centre's biggest engineering jobs.",
      "Every server turns electricity into heat, and thousands packed together could quickly cook themselves. Many data centres arrange racks in alternating hot and cold aisles: cool air is pushed through the front of the servers and hot air is captured from the back. Large chillers, fans and cooling towers then remove the heat.\n\nSome facilities use water to cool, which raises concerns in dry regions. Others pump liquid directly to the hottest chips, an approach that has become more common as artificial intelligence hardware runs hotter. Some companies build in cold climates, such as the Nordic countries, to use chilly outside air, and a few have experimented with reusing waste heat to warm nearby homes."],
    ["Power and copies", "Backup power keeps data centres running through blackouts, and multiple copies of your data mean one failure does not lose it.",
      "A data centre cannot switch off during a storm. If the grid supply flickers, banks of batteries in an uninterruptible power supply take over instantly. Within seconds to minutes, large diesel or gas generators start and carry the load for as long as their fuel lasts. The system is tested regularly.\n\nHard drives fail all the time, and in a building with thousands of them, some fail every day. So an uploaded photo is typically stored as several copies, or split into pieces with extra error-correcting information, across different drives and machines. Important services also copy data to separate buildings or regions. This redundancy is why a cloud backup is generally safer than one external drive in a drawer. Still, accounts get hacked and services shut down, so keep precious files in more than one place."],
    ["Content close to home", "Content delivery networks keep copies of popular content on servers near users so it loads faster.",
      "When you press play on a popular show, the video probably does not travel from a central data centre overseas. Streaming services and many websites use content delivery networks, which keep copies of popular content on servers close to where people live, including in Australian cities and inside some internet providers' networks.\n\nYour request is quietly directed to the nearest suitable server, so the video starts faster and long international links are not clogged with millions of identical streams. The same trick speeds up news sites, software updates and online games.\n\nThis matters in Australia, which is far from many of the world's big data hubs, so every round trip to the United States or Europe adds a noticeable delay."],
    ["The cost of convenience", "Data centres use roughly one to two percent of the world's electricity, and where your data is stored can matter legally.",
      "Convenience has a footprint. Estimates from bodies such as the International Energy Agency suggest data centres use somewhere around one to two percent of the world's electricity, and demand is rising quickly as artificial intelligence grows. Big cloud companies have signed deals to buy large amounts of renewable energy, though how green any particular data centre is can be complicated.\n\nIn Australia, Sydney and Melbourne host most data centres, and new projects compete for grid connections and land. Location also matters legally. Data stored in another country may be subject to that country's laws, so governments, banks and health services often require sensitive information to stay onshore. As a user, delete things you no longer need, and check where and how a service stores your data, especially anything personal or confidential."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does 'the cloud' really refer to?", ["Data stored in satellites","Wireless signals in the air","A special part of your phone's memory","Computers owned by another company"], 3, "Your files sit on physical machines in data centres that you will probably never see."],
    [1, "What is the purpose of hot and cold aisles in a data centre?", ["To manage airflow so servers stay cool","To separate staff from visitors","To store spare parts","To divide different customers' data"], 0, "Cool air enters the front of the servers and hot air is captured from the back."],
    [2, "Why do cloud services keep multiple copies of your data?", ["To charge you more for storage","So a failed drive does not lose data","To make files load in colour","Because the law forbids single copies"], 1, "Redundancy protects against drive failures, fires and outages."],
    [3, "Why do streaming services use content delivery networks?", ["To encrypt every customer's passwords","To block unwanted online advertising","To reduce the heat from servers","To store popular content near viewers"], 3, "Nearby copies start faster and avoid clogging long international links."],
    [4, "Roughly what share of the world's electricity do data centres use, according to estimates?", ["About one to two percent","About ten percent","About a third","Less than one hundredth of one percent"], 0, "The share is modest but rising quickly as artificial intelligence grows."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your photos are not floating in the sky. Here is where they actually sit."});

WB.L("lithium-ion-batteries", "technology", "beginner", "Inside the battery in your pocket",
  "Lithium-ion batteries power your phone, laptop, cordless drill and maybe your car. Knowing how they work explains why they slowly fade, and how to make them last longer.",
  [
    ["A tiny chemical shuttle", "A lithium-ion battery works by shuttling lithium ions between two electrodes while electrons do useful work through your device.",
      "Every time you unplug your phone, you carry a small chemical machine. A lithium-ion battery has an anode, usually graphite, and a cathode made from a lithium compound with metals such as cobalt, nickel, manganese or iron. Between them sit an electrolyte, which lets lithium ions pass, and a thin porous separator that stops the electrodes touching.\n\nWhen the battery powers your phone, lithium ions travel through the electrolyte from anode to cathode. Electrons take the long way round, through the phone's circuits. That flow of electrons is the current doing useful work. Charging pushes everything back the other way."],
    ["Why lithium?", "Lithium is the lightest metal and gives up electrons easily, so it stores a lot of energy for its weight.",
      "Lithium is the lightest metal, and it is eager to give up an electron. Together, those traits let a battery store plenty of energy without much weight.\n\nCompared with older rechargeables such as nickel cadmium, lithium-ion batteries hold far more energy for their size and lose little charge sitting on a shelf. They also avoid the memory effect that once led people to drain batteries fully before recharging. That combination made truly portable electronics possible.\n\nAustralia has a direct stake in this story. It is one of the world's largest miners of lithium, much of it dug from hard rock deposits in Western Australia and shipped overseas for processing."],
    ["A Nobel-winning invention", "The lithium-ion battery took decades and many researchers, and three of them shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.",
      "In the 1970s, during the oil crisis, British chemist M. Stanley Whittingham built an early rechargeable lithium battery. It used pure lithium metal and could be dangerous.\n\nAround 1980, American physicist John Goodenough, working at Oxford, found a cobalt oxide cathode with much higher voltage. In the mid 1980s, Japanese chemist Akira Yoshino swapped the reactive lithium metal for a carbon-based anode, creating a far safer design.\n\nSony sold the first commercial lithium-ion batteries in 1991, in a video camera. The three scientists shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Goodenough was 97, the oldest person ever to receive a Nobel Prize."],
    ["Fading and charging smarter", "Batteries fade through chemistry that heat speeds up, so keep devices cool and avoid long stretches at completely full or completely empty.",
      "Your battery holds less charge after a couple of years because of chemistry, not a conspiracy. Each cycle swells and shrinks the electrodes, and side reactions use up lithium. A crust grows on the anode and traps lithium that can no longer carry charge. Heat speeds this up, so a hot car dashboard in an Australian summer is especially bad. Long periods at completely full or empty add stress.\n\nPhones charge fast to about 80 percent, then slow down on purpose to avoid damage. Partial top-ups are fine. Keeping charge roughly between 20 and 80 percent helps, and optimised charging settings do this for you. Use reputable chargers. Cold only reduces performance temporarily."],
    ["Safety and disposal", "A damaged lithium-ion battery can overheat and catch fire, so stop using swollen or hot batteries and never put used ones in household bins.",
      "Lithium-ion batteries are generally very safe, but they pack a lot of energy and a flammable electrolyte. If one is crushed, punctured, badly made or overcharged without protection, an internal short circuit can heat it. That heat triggers more reactions in a spiral called thermal runaway, which can cause fire. These fires are hard to put out and can reignite. Warning signs are swelling, hissing, unusual heat or a strange smell. Stop using and charging the device, and check your fire service's advice.\n\nCrushed batteries cause fires in garbage trucks and recycling centres. Keep them out of kerbside bins. Use a drop-off point, such as the B-cycle scheme's supermarket and hardware store collection points, and tape the terminals first."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happens inside the battery as it powers your phone?", ["Electrons travel through the electrolyte while lithium ions travel through the circuits","The lithium is burned off gradually to release heat that becomes electricity","Lithium ions move from the anode to the cathode while electrons travel through the circuits","The separator slowly dissolves and releases stored charge into the phone"], 2, "Ions move through the electrolyte and electrons take the outside route, and that flow of electrons is the current."],
    [1, "Why is lithium so well suited to portable batteries?", ["It is very light and gives up electrons easily","It is very dense and holds charge by sheer weight","It is cheap and never needs to be mined","It reacts slowly, so it loses charge on a shelf"], 0, "Being the lightest metal and an eager electron donor lets lithium store lots of energy for little weight."],
    [2, "Why was replacing lithium metal with a carbon-based anode important?", ["It doubled the voltage of the cell","It let the battery charge in seconds","It removed the need for an electrolyte","It made the battery much safer"], 3, "Pure lithium metal is reactive and could be dangerous, so a carbon anode produced a far safer design."],
    [3, "Which habit is most likely to help your phone battery last longer?", ["Draining it to zero every time before recharging","Keeping it cool and avoiding long spells flat or full","Leaving it on a car dashboard to stay warm","Only ever charging it when it is completely empty"], 1, "Heat and long stretches at the extremes stress the battery, and partial charges are fine."],
    [4, "What should you do with a used lithium-ion battery?", ["Put it in the kerbside recycling bin","Put it in the general rubbish if undamaged","Take it to a battery drop-off point","Keep it somewhere hot until it is flat"], 2, "Batteries crushed in bin trucks can ignite, so use a drop-off point such as the B-cycle scheme."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your phone's battery is fading, and heat is the quiet culprit. Here's the chemistry."});

WB.L("undersea-cables", "technology", "intermediate", "The internet lives under the sea",
  "When you video call someone overseas, your voice almost certainly travels as flashes of light through a cable lying on the ocean floor. These hidden lines carry the vast majority of the world's international data.",
  [
    ["Cables, not satellites", "Almost all international internet data travels through cables on the sea floor, not satellites.",
      "Ask people how the internet crosses oceans and many guess satellites. Satellites matter for remote areas, ships and planes, but the overwhelming majority of data between continents travels through cables on the sea floor.\n\nSeveral hundred submarine cables are in service, stretching well over a million kilometres in total. When you email London, join a meeting with colleagues in Singapore or watch an overseas video that isn't cached locally, your data races through them. Cables are faster, carry vastly more data and cost far less per bit than satellites, which is why they dominate."],
    ["Light in a glass thread", "Submarine cables carry data as pulses of laser light along hair-thin glass fibres, boosted along the way by repeaters.",
      "At the heart of a cable are a handful of optical fibres, strands of extremely pure glass about as thin as a human hair. Lasers send pulses of light down each one, and the light stays inside by reflecting off the glass boundary. Modern systems send many colours down the same fibre at once, each carrying its own stream.\n\nLayers of protection surround the fibres: gel, steel wires, a copper layer to carry power and plastic insulation. In the deep ocean, a cable may be only as thick as a garden hose.\n\nLight fades over distance, so repeaters spaced roughly 50 to 100 kilometres apart boost it with optical amplifiers. Power reaches them along the copper layer from shore stations."],
    ["Laying a cable", "Cables are laid by specialised ships along a surveyed route, and are armoured and buried near shore where anchors and fishing gear pose a risk.",
      "Before laying, surveyors map the seabed and avoid steep slopes, rocky ridges, undersea volcanoes and busy anchorages. Specialised cable ships then carry thousands of kilometres of cable coiled in huge tanks.\n\nIn deep water, the ship slowly pays out the cable, which sinks to rest on the sea floor, sometimes more than eight kilometres down. Closer to shore, where fishing and anchors pose dangers, the cable is thicker and more heavily armoured, and a plough often buries it in a trench. It comes ashore at a landing station.\n\nA long cable can take many months to lay, and projects often cost hundreds of millions of dollars."],
    ["Breaks and repairs", "Cables break often, mostly from human activity rather than sharks, and traffic is rerouted while repair ships fix them.",
      "Cables do break. Industry groups estimate well over a hundred faults worldwide each year. Most come from people, particularly fishing trawlers dragging gear and ships dropping or dragging anchors. Earthquakes and underwater landslides cause others. Sharks have been filmed biting cables, but they are not a significant cause of damage.\n\nWhen a fault occurs, traffic is usually rerouted over other cables, so users may only notice slower speeds. A repair ship then finds the break, lifts the cable with a grappling hook or a remotely operated vehicle, splices in a new section on deck and lowers it back. Many countries protect cable zones near their coasts."],
    ["A Victorian beginning", "Undersea cables are older than the internet: telegraph cables cut message times between Australia and Britain from weeks to hours, and Australia still depends on them.",
      "In 1858, a telegraph cable across the Atlantic let Queen Victoria and US President James Buchanan exchange messages. It failed within weeks, partly because operators applied too much voltage. In 1866, the steamship Great Eastern laid a durable one. Insulation was gutta percha, a natural latex.\n\nAustralia joined the network in 1872, when the Overland Telegraph from Adelaide to Darwin linked to an undersea cable north toward Java. Messages that took weeks by ship now arrived within hours.\n\nToday almost all of Australia's international traffic still depends on cables, landing mainly near Sydney and Perth, plus Darwin and the Sunshine Coast. Protection zones restrict anchoring and some fishing. Tech giants now help fund new cables, and governments treat them as critical infrastructure."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does most international internet traffic cross the oceans?", ["Through satellites orbiting above the Earth","Through cables laid on the sea floor","Through radio towers on ships","Through cables strung between islands above water"], 1, "Cables are faster, carry far more data and cost less per bit than satellites, so they dominate."],
    [1, "Why do long submarine cables include repeaters?", ["The glass fibres cannot carry any light more than a few metres","They convert the signal into radio waves for the final stretch","They stop sharks from biting the cable","Light fades over distance, so the signal needs boosting"], 3, "Even pure glass dims the light gradually, so powered repeaters amplify it every 50 to 100 kilometres or so."],
    [2, "Why is a cable thicker and often buried near the shore?", ["Fishing gear and anchors are a danger in shallow water","The water is colder there and the glass would crack","Ships need to lift it for inspection each year","Waves would otherwise wash it into the open ocean"], 0, "The deep sea is relatively undisturbed, so protection is added where human activity is heavy."],
    [3, "What causes most submarine cable faults?", ["Sharks biting through the cable","Overheating of the glass fibres","Trawler nets and ship anchors","Solar storms disrupting the light"], 2, "Fishing gear and anchors cause most breaks, while sharks are not a significant cause."],
    [4, "What changed for Australia when the 1872 telegraph link opened?", ["Australia gained its first radio broadcasts","Letters to Britain stopped being sent by ship","A satellite link connected Darwin and Java","Messages to Britain took hours, not weeks"], 3, "Telegraph cables cut communication times between Australia and Britain from weeks to hours."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your video call to London doesn't go through space. It goes through the ocean floor."});

WB.L("babbage-and-lovelace", "technology", "intermediate", "Babbage, Lovelace and the first computer design",
  "In the 1830s, long before electricity was in homes, a cranky English mathematician designed a programmable computer made of brass gears. A young countess saw what it could become.",
  [
    ["Tables full of mistakes", "Babbage wanted machines to calculate error-free mathematical tables, because human 'computers' and typesetters let mistakes creep in.",
      "In the early 1800s, sailors, engineers, astronomers and bankers relied on thick books of mathematical tables: logarithms, tide times, star positions and interest calculations. People called 'computers', a job title long before it meant a machine, worked them out, and typesetters copied them by hand. Errors crept in at every stage, and a wrong digit in a navigation table could put a ship on the rocks.\n\nCharles Babbage, a brilliant and famously argumentative English mathematician, was frustrated. By his own later story, while checking tables with his friend John Herschel, he wished steam could do the calculations."],
    ["The Difference Engine", "The Difference Engine turned table-making into repeated addition that gears could do, but it was never completed in Babbage's time.",
      "In the 1820s, Babbage designed a machine to produce tables mechanically. It used the method of differences, which turns many formulas into repeated addition, something gears can do reliably. It would even print results, removing copying errors.\n\nThe British government funded it, and Babbage hired a skilled engineer, Joseph Clement. A small working section impressed visitors. But the machine needed thousands of precisely machined parts, and disputes over money and tool ownership plagued the project. Work stopped in the early 1830s.\n\nBy then Babbage had become distracted by a far more ambitious idea."],
    ["The Analytical Engine", "The Analytical Engine was a general-purpose design with memory, a processor, punched-card instructions and decision-making, which makes it the first design for a computer.",
      "The Analytical Engine was designed to carry out any calculation. It had a 'store' for holding numbers and a 'mill' for arithmetic, roughly what we now call memory and a processor. Instructions came on punched cards, an idea borrowed from the Jacquard loom, which wove complex patterns into silk.\n\nCrucially, the machine could choose what to do next based on its own results, and repeat sets of instructions. Those features mean it could follow programs, the defining trait of a computer.\n\nBabbage refined the design for decades, but the Analytical Engine was never built."],
    ["Ada Lovelace's notes", "Ada Lovelace's 1843 notes gave the clearest account of the Analytical Engine, including a step-by-step method often called the first published computer program.",
      "Augusta Ada Byron was born in 1815, the only legitimate child of the poet Lord Byron, who left when she was a baby. Her mother ensured she had an unusually strong education in mathematics and science. Around seventeen, Ada met Babbage and saw part of the Difference Engine. She was captivated, and later, as Countess of Lovelace, kept up a lively correspondence with him.\n\nIn 1842, Luigi Menabrea published a paper describing the Analytical Engine. Lovelace translated it and added notes about three times longer. In Note G she set out a method to calculate the Bernoulli numbers, often called the first published computer program. Historians debate how much was Babbage's, but her notes were the clearest account of the time."],
    ["Beyond numbers", "Lovelace saw that a computer could work on any symbols, not just numbers, and in 1991 a working Difference Engine proved Babbage's design sound.",
      "Babbage mostly saw a powerful calculator. Lovelace saw more: the engine could manipulate any symbols whose relationships follow rules. If musical sounds could be captured that way, she speculated, it might compose music. She also cautioned that it could only do what it was instructed to do. In 1950, Alan Turing called this 'Lady Lovelace's objection'. She died in 1852, aged 36.\n\nWas Babbage too far ahead of his time, or were his designs flawed? The Science Museum in London built his Difference Engine No. 2 from his drawings, using tolerances achievable in his era. Finished in 1991, it worked. A programming language, Ada, is named for her."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What problem was Babbage trying to solve?", ["A shortage of mathematicians to teach navigation","The high cost of printing books of tables","Slow postal delivery of astronomical data","Errors in hand-calculated, hand-copied tables"], 3, "Errors crept in at every stage of making tables, and mistakes could have serious consequences such as shipwrecks."],
    [1, "Why were gears able to calculate tables in the Difference Engine?", ["Steam power solved the equations directly","Many formulas reduce to repeated addition","Every possible answer was stored in advance","Punched cards were used to guess results"], 1, "Repeated addition is something gears can do reliably, which made mechanical table-making possible."],
    [2, "What made the Analytical Engine a design for a true computer?", ["It printed tables faster than the Difference Engine","It was powered by steam rather than by hand","It could run programs with decisions and repeats","It was built from more gears than any earlier machine"], 2, "Being able to choose what to do next and repeat instructions means a machine can follow programs."],
    [3, "Why are Lovelace's notes so significant?", ["They clearly explained the engine and a program for it","They proved the machine was impossible to build","They were a translation without any new ideas","They replaced Babbage's designs with her own machine"], 0, "Note G set out a detailed method for the engine, often called the first published computer program."],
    [4, "What was Lovelace's most remarkable insight?", ["Engines would one day run on electricity","Computers would always need punched cards","Machines could handle any rule-based symbols","A machine could think and originate ideas"], 2, "She saw that computing could extend beyond arithmetic to anything expressible in rules, such as music."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A countess saw a use for the first computer design that its inventor missed."});

WB.L("file-compression", "technology", "advanced", "How files get squeezed: the art of compression",
  "A whole CD's worth of music can shrink to about a tenth of its size, so thousands of songs fit on your phone. Compression works by finding patterns, and by cleverly throwing away what your eyes and ears will not miss.",
  [
    ["Making files smaller", "Compression stores the same information in fewer bits, either perfectly rebuilt (lossless) or close enough (lossy).",
      "One minute of uncompressed CD-quality stereo audio takes about ten megabytes. Uncompressed high definition video would need hundreds of megabytes every few seconds, far more than a typical home connection could carry. Yet you stream films on a train and store thousands of photos on a phone.\n\nThe trick is compression: representing the same information, or something close enough, in fewer bits. Lossless compression shrinks a file so it can be rebuilt perfectly, bit for bit. Lossy compression goes further by discarding details people are unlikely to notice. Most media you enjoy relies on clever lossy methods built on top of lossless ideas."],
    ["Patterns and short codes", "Lossless compression exploits repetition and gives common symbols shorter codes, but it cannot shrink truly random data.",
      "Picture a clear blue sky, where a row holds a thousand identical blue pixels. Instead of storing blue a thousand times, store 'blue, repeated 1,000 times'. This is run length encoding. A more powerful method from Abraham Lempel and Jacob Ziv in the late 1970s replaces repeated sequences with a short pointer: 'go back this far and copy this many characters'. Variations power ZIP files, PNG images and web page transfers.\n\nMorse code already used another idea: the common letter E is one dot, while rare Q gets a long pattern. In 1952, David Huffman, then an MIT student, published a method for building the best such code. Truly random data cannot be shrunk at all."],
    ["How JPEG fools your eyes", "JPEG saves space by discarding fine detail and colour precision that human vision barely notices.",
      "Lossy image compression exploits quirks of human vision. JPEG, a standard from the early 1990s, first separates brightness from colour. Our eyes are far more sensitive to brightness differences than colour ones, so JPEG usually stores colour at lower resolution.\n\nNext it splits the image into small blocks, typically eight by eight pixels, and describes each as a mix of smooth patterns, from broad gradients to fine stripes. The fine, busy patterns matter least to our eyes, so they are rounded off heavily or thrown away. Lossless coding then packs what remains.\n\nPush the quality setting down and the trick shows: blurry edges, smudged colours and visible square blocks."],
    ["How MP3 fools your ears", "MP3 and AAC shrink audio by dropping or skimping on sounds your hearing would mask or miss.",
      "Audio formats such as MP3, developed in Germany in the late 1980s and early 1990s, and the later AAC, use psychoacoustics, the science of how we perceive sound. We cannot hear very high or very low frequencies well, and a loud sound masks quieter sounds at nearby frequencies.\n\nAn encoder analyses music in short slices, works out which parts would be masked or inaudible, and spends fewer bits on them or drops them. The result can be roughly a tenth the size of the original CD audio while sounding very similar to most listeners.\n\nAt low bit rates, you may hear swirly, watery artefacts, particularly in cymbals and applause."],
    ["Video and lost generations", "Video codecs send only what changes between frames, and repeated lossy saving degrades quality, so keep an original copy.",
      "Consecutive video frames are usually very similar. In a newsreader shot, the background barely changes. Codecs such as H.264, H.265 and AV1 store full keyframes only occasionally, and describe how blocks have moved since an earlier frame, plus small corrections. When data goes missing, streams turn into smeared blocks because movement is applied to the wrong picture until the next keyframe. Confetti, rain and fast panning are hard to compress.\n\nLossy formats also cause generation loss. Each time you edit and re-save a JPEG, or share a photo through apps that recompress it, more detail is lost. Keep an original of anything important, and make compressed copies from that master."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the difference between lossless and lossy compression?", ["Lossless discards detail, while lossy keeps every bit","Lossless is only for video, lossy only for audio","Lossless rebuilds exactly, lossy discards some detail","Lossless makes files bigger, lossy makes them smaller"], 2, "Lossless rebuilds the original bit for bit, whereas lossy accepts small, hard-to-notice changes for smaller files."],
    [1, "How does run length encoding shrink a row of a thousand identical blue pixels?", ["It stores 'blue, repeated 1,000 times'","It keeps one blue pixel and deletes the rest","It converts the blue to grey to save space","It stores the row twice and compares them"], 0, "Describing a repetition instead of storing each copy is the core idea behind many lossless methods."],
    [2, "Why can JPEG store colour at lower resolution than brightness?", ["Cameras record colour less accurately than brightness","Colour takes more bits than brightness, so it is cut","Screens cannot display fine colour detail","Our eyes notice brightness far more than colour"], 3, "JPEG exploits human vision by spending its bits where our eyes notice most."],
    [3, "What does an MP3 encoder do to make audio smaller?", ["Removes every quiet sound from the track","Drops sounds your ears would miss","Lowers the volume of the whole track","Stores the track at half speed"], 1, "Psychoacoustics tells the encoder which parts you won't hear, so those get fewer bits or none."],
    [4, "Why do photos grow blurrier when shared through several messaging apps?", ["The apps gradually shrink the image's pixel count to save battery","Photos weaken when they travel over the internet","Each share adds a layer of noise to the original","Each app recompresses the image and discards more detail"], 3, "Repeated lossy compression is generation loss, so make copies from the original master."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A whole CD fits in a tenth of the space, and you can't hear what was thrown away."});

WB.L("making-microchips", "technology", "advanced", "From sand to silicon: how microchips are made",
  "The chip in your phone holds billions of switches, each smaller than most viruses. Making one is arguably the most precise manufacturing humans have ever attempted.",
  [
    ["Billions of switches", "A chip's transistors are microscopic switches, and a modern phone processor packs over ten billion into a fingernail-sized chip.",
      "Every app you open and message you send is processed by transistors, microscopic switches that turn current on and off. Combine enough of them in the right patterns and they can add numbers, compare values, store data and run any program.\n\nThe first transistor, built at Bell Labs in 1947, was big enough to see and handle. Today a high end phone processor packs well over ten billion onto a chip roughly the size of a fingernail. The features are only tens of atoms across, layered with astonishing accuracy across a whole silicon disc at once. The process can take a few months from bare wafer to finished chip."],
    ["From sand to wafer", "Chips begin as sand refined into silicon of extraordinary purity and grown into a single giant crystal, then sliced into wafers.",
      "Silicon is the second most abundant element in Earth's crust, found in quartz sand and rock. But chips need silicon purer than almost anything else people make, with impurities around one atom in a billion.\n\nPurified silicon is melted. A seed crystal is dipped in and slowly drawn upward while rotating, so the silicon solidifies into one perfect crystal called an ingot, a technique named after Polish scientist Jan Czochralski. Modern ingots can be as wide as a dinner plate and around two metres long. Diamond saws slice them into wafers less than a millimetre thick, polished until flatter than almost any other surface."],
    ["Printing with light", "Photolithography prints circuit patterns onto a wafer with light, then etches, adds or alters material, layer after layer in a dust-free cleanroom.",
      "The key technique is photolithography. The wafer is coated with a light sensitive chemical, photoresist. Light shines through a mask carrying the circuit pattern and is focused through lenses onto the wafer. Wherever light hits, the photoresist changes, so it can be washed away. The exposed areas can then be etched, have material added, or be bombarded with other atoms to change how they conduct electricity, called doping. The machine steps across the wafer, printing the pattern for hundreds of chips.\n\nA chip has hundreds or even over a thousand such steps. One dust particle can destroy a chip, so fabs use cleanrooms with air thousands of times cleaner than a hospital operating theatre. Not every chip works. The share that does is the yield."],
    ["Extreme ultraviolet", "The most advanced chips are printed with extreme ultraviolet light, made by firing lasers at tin droplets, in machines only one company builds.",
      "The smaller the wavelength, the finer the patterns that can be printed. For years, chipmakers used deep ultraviolet light and tricks such as exposing the same layer several times. The most advanced chips now use extreme ultraviolet, or EUV, at 13.5 nanometres.\n\nMaking it is extraordinary. A powerful laser hits tiny droplets of molten tin tens of thousands of times a second, creating a plasma that emits EUV light. Air and glass absorb EUV, so the beam travels in a vacuum and is steered by ultra smooth mirrors rather than lenses.\n\nOnly one company, ASML in the Netherlands, makes these machines. Each is about the size of a bus and costs hundreds of millions of dollars."],
    ["Moore and the chain", "Chip progress is slowing past Moore's law, and no single country makes a modern chip from start to finish.",
      "In 1965, Gordon Moore, later a co-founder of Intel, noticed the number of components on a chip was doubling regularly. In 1975 he revised this to roughly every two years. Moore's law held for decades, but the pace has slowed. Names like '3 nanometre' are now mostly labels for each generation, not real measurements, so chipmakers stack components and redesign transistors instead.\n\nNo single country makes a modern chip alone. Designs often come from the United States, the most advanced manufacturing is concentrated in Taiwan, especially at TSMC, EUV machines come from the Netherlands, and specialised materials from Japan, South Korea and Europe. The shortages after 2020 showed how fragile this chain is, and governments are now spending heavily on local factories."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does a transistor do?", ["Stores electricity like a tiny battery for later use","Acts as a microscopic switch that turns current on and off","Converts heat from the chip into light","Amplifies Wi-Fi signals to the phone's antenna"], 1, "Billions of switches arranged in the right patterns can add, compare, store data and run programs."],
    [1, "Why must the silicon used for chips be so pure?", ["Sand is too coarse to be used in electronics","Pure silicon is cheaper to slice into wafers","Impurities make the wafer too heavy to polish","Tiny impurities would disrupt the crystal"], 3, "Chips need silicon purer than almost anything else people make, with impurities around one atom in a billion."],
    [2, "Why do chip factories use cleanrooms?", ["A single dust particle can destroy a chip","Workers need protection from harmful chemicals only","Light would otherwise make the wafers glow","Robots cannot work in normal air"], 0, "Features are so small that one speck of dust can ruin a chip, so cleanroom air is thousands of times cleaner than a theatre."],
    [3, "Why does EUV light travel in a vacuum and bounce off mirrors?", ["Mirrors make the light brighter than lenses do","Vacuums let the tin droplets stay molten","Air and glass absorb EUV light","Lenses would melt in the laser's heat"], 2, "Because EUV is absorbed by air and glass, machines use a vacuum and ultra smooth mirrors."],
    [4, "What does a chip labelled '3 nanometre' actually tell you?", ["Every feature on the chip is exactly three nanometres wide","It names a generation, not a real measurement","The chip is three nanometres thick","The chip was printed with three nanometre light"], 1, "These names once related to real dimensions but are now mostly labels for each new generation."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your phone's chip is made with light, molten tin and the cleanest air on Earth."});

